 a script or a conversation and  making demos of it that they can listen  to or they can shoot to or edit to and  there's really a great advantage to that  because I find that more often than not  the music that I write before the film  is being shot ends up in the movie and  and to a large extent plays a very big  role on the movie so I always think of a  director's first experience hearing  music is like when a goose is born and  they imprint on whatever live person or  creature they see as being their parent  and so I like to think the director  first hears my music in their movie and  that's the music they're gonna stick  with if you don't really think about it  in terms of images in the beginning and  you really just think about music that's  what I try and tell young composers is  just focus on music in the beginning  write something that it has a little bit  of substance to it then turn on the  movie and play your music and see if  some of it works and I bet probably a  lot of it will I make very elaborate  demos and I think most people do now I  mean that's just kind of that's been  part of my process I mean you really  need to be familiar with some some kind  of software with for electronic music to  create demos of what the musics gonna  sound like for the director so by the  time that we get into the recording  studio with a giant Orchestra the  directors heard everything and we've  sort of fought most of our battles  I'm extremely hands-on I don't conduct  during my recording because I like to be  in the control room and I really am very  protective of that environment I want to  make sure that every time there's a  playback I have all my pre-record 'he's  the synthesizers the chorus they've done  everything balanced properly because if  it if it's not balanced properly  director can become insecure about well  that cue doesn't sound like it used to  what's wrong so I make sure that every  time there's a playback it sounds  exactly is it supposed to sound  fantastic bees was a situation where  there was a there was a vocabulary  pretty much already established by a guy  named John Williams and wrote some  pretty memorable themes so I I knew that  this was not a Harry Potter movie but it  was in the Harry Potter universe so my  feeling was that he was going to it  still need to be in the same vocabulary  I wanted to get rid of Hedwig steam as  quick as I could and have my own themes  I knew it was going to have to be kind  of sophisticated orchestral writing with  a lot of sort of magical elements and  that always involves a lot of note  writing I was really focused on the  themes and I wrote all of the themes for  the movie before I saw any other movie  and started communicating with David  Yates very early on and we went through  a lot of explorations and I think we all  made the right decision before we were  all  I hope I think it's very hard to keep  things fresh over a you know six or  seven month period which is what some of  these movies are I know I was on  fantastic bees for instance from  November of 16 through September of 17  so that's a lot of time a lot of writing  I'm not sure I think I think if you've  written a good if you have a good  approach it will survive all the the  audience previews and the focus groups  and the in the reiterations and the  constant scrutiny I think what doesn't  survive is it is probably best to just  leave by the by the side by the wayside  plus I have a couple of people around me  that I think are really objective they  know my work really well they know me  really well and of course there's a  collaboration between myself and a  filmmaker that is extremely important  and you know it all it's a team effort  it's it's it's total collaboration  otherwise it's it just doesn't work well  I I you know gosh  I I trying to remember John I got a call  from John Wells who was the executive  producer on ER and he asked me if I  wanted to do a pilot I think he had done  something before that made me think that  this was going to be good and he  described some actor named George  Clooney and these different people I  didn't do much TV back then I was pretty  much doing features and I said sure I'll  do the I'll do the pilot but I don't  want to do the series I'll do the pilot  and then I'm gonna hand it off to my  friend and we did it that way and I  wrote you know I hate to say I hate to  destroy everybody's fantasies and pop  their bubbles but I wrote it in about 15  minutes when you have a good idea it  comes very quickly and it comes kind of  in it in a total complete form so it was  you know I I knew it had to have a heart  I knew it had to feel like an emergency  to some extent in the front you know  it's just I got lucky  other than the original inception in the  original you know writing of thematic  material and the idea and the feeling of  the movie I think the most difficult  thing is rewriting without question you  know a rewriting is is devastating you  know you just feel like you've been  kicked in the gut and you can't imagine  that somebody could be so stupid as to  not understand how right you are  and for you know a longest period of  time that was my attitude I'd say for  the first 10 years I was doing movies I  I felt I knew everything and I didn't  really care what the director had to say  which is really ridiculous and and then  I realized that a lot of times if not  most of the time a director has insights  into the project that I could never have  they've been on it they were involved  with getting the things started they had  they had to tell this story it meant  they meant the world to them and there  are things about it that I need to know  and I realized that part of my  resistance to rewriting had to do with  just physical laziness and I think  everybody must feel that way  you just think can I get it up again to  be that enthusiastic that passionate  that can I believe in any other idea and  the truth is almost without fail every  time I do a rewrite and I feel like I'm  in a clear kind of collaboration and  communication with the director it's  better so I've learned to just take a  deep breath quickly and be thick skins  about thick skinned about it and  understand that rewriting is a big part  of the job to get in this business is a  very difficult thing to begin with and I  always say have a plan B I mean it may  be it it might work out and it might not  it's it if it's going to work out first  of all you're gonna have to stick to it  and be persistent for a long time  possibly be good at some kind of ability  to create electronic music you know be  able to make a good sounding demo lie  yourself with young filmmakers student  filmmakers do it you know pay to pay to  compose music for somebody do whatever  you have to do just to get your music  attached to some visual but I would say  more than anything make sure that your  the the demo you make sounds good  because the technology is so pervasive  now that every mate everybody makes a  good sounding demo they may not have any  substance to it but sometimes a director  can't tell the difference so make sure  that the at least that the music sounds  good that it has good sounding samples  and those can be it acquired fairly  cheaply these days and then just be very  friendly and be willing to rewrite a  whole bunch of times  gosh I didn't really know much about the  business I mean I when I started I you  know I was lucky enough to have an agent  and I think  you need to ally yourself with somebody  you trust who can represent you in all  those areas probably make yourself a  deal with the Performing Rights  Association BMI ASCAP those kinds of  things CSEC and I would protect your  music III would be careful about who you  who you give it to I wouldn't be afraid  to actually give it to somebody and say  you can use my music for free in this  movie because they don't have any money  and they don't they can't pay you but in  a way using your music in a film that  you think is worthy is payment and I  wouldn't get too hung up on the money in  the beginning but you do want to you  don't want to lose the rights to your  music if at all possible and so you  probably need to at least have a  conversation with an attorney if you  start to get an opportunity like that in  the beginning probably I would have I  would have been more relaxed about it I  would have known that it was somehow  always going to get done because I'm  such a hyper responsible student if you  will and when I get an assignment I'm  just I just you know become crazed that  I have to get it done in a certain  amount of time and it's funny when I was  working with Hans Zimmer on our first  Batman Begins collaboration and we were  four or five days away from the first  orchestra date and we hadn't sent  anything out to any orchestrators and I  said Hans you know you're crazy we're  sitting here four days away from  recording we have no orchestrations he  said what day is it I said it's Thursday  say when do we start I said Monday said  we have plenty of time and when he said  that to me I laughed and I realized that  you know composing is supposed to be fun  even when it's difficult and you're  rewriting and you want to pull your hair  out somehow find a way for it to be fun  and appreciate how lucky you are to get  paid to write music  [Music]

